1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Be3 Qf6 6. c3 Nge7 7. Bb5 d6 8.
O-O O-O 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Bxc5 cxb5 11. Bd4 Qg6 12. Qf3 Bb7 13. Nd2 c5 14. Be3
Nc6 15. Qg3 Rad8 16. f4 Qxg3 17. hxg3 f5 18. Rad1 fxe4 19. Nxe4 Rfe8 20. Nxd6
Rxd6 21. Rxd6 Rxe3 22. Kf2 Re7 23. Rd5 Kf7 24. Rxc5 Rd7 25. Re1 Rd2+ 26. Re2
Rxe2+ 27. Kxe2 {"Shortly afterwards, 4.52, a draw was proposed and accepted on
Board 1, the game having advanced to a simple ending, in which White had a
Rook and two extra Pawns - one on each wing - for two minor pieces" (also BCM,
1883, p140) "... progressing with commendable caution till the 20th move, when
Locock won, through a series of exchanges, two minor pieces for a rook; but
Morley obtained two pawns, which equalised the game so far that both players
agreed to give it up as drawn on the 27th move, after two hours and three
quarters' play..." (The Field, 24 March 1883) Source: Glasgow Weekly Herald,
31 March 1883. Contributed by Brian Denman, 22 May 2021} 1/2-1/2

