{"... Dr. Zukertort now adjudicated and though he preferred Black's position
was unable to decide in his favour; and the game was drawn. Throughout both
games the two players aimed at securing position rather than attack and being
apparently well-matched their efforts nearly neutralised one another. Their
first game, as was to be expected, was the steadiest and best game of the
match." BCM, 1883, p140. "In the second game Morley adopted the French defence
against Locock. Contrary to expectation, the game turned out a very
interesting one, both combatants display a great deal of ingenuity, thus
compensating to a great extent for the dulness [sic] of the first game. It is
very much to be regretted that the progress of the contest had to be
interrupted at 6.30pm when time was called, and the umpire had to give his
decision on the following position, the verdict being - drawn game." (The
Field, 24 March 1883)} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nc6 {(?)} 4. Nf3 {Black had
prematurely blocked his c-pawn: White should, therefore, proceed with 4 e5,
being now in the position to hold the centre. (there is a move number error in
the text: "5 P to K5")} Bb4 5. exd5 exd5 6. Bd3 Nf6 7. O-O Bxc3 8. bxc3 O-O 9.
Re1 (9. c4 {would be vastly preferable.}) 9... Bg4 10. Rb1 b6 11. Bb5 ({
Weak again: White should continue with} 11. Ba3) 11... Qd6 12. Rb3 ({Instead
of displacing the Rook, he should proceed with} 12. Qd3) 12... Rfe8 13. Rf1 a6
14. Ba3 {(?)} Qe6 15. Bxc6 Qxc6 16. Qd3 Bxf3 17. Qxf3 Ne4 18. Qd3 a5 19. f3 {
(?)} a4 {adjudicated a draw by Zukertort (n.b. engines give a win for Black
but see the note after Black's move 25...Qh3 to see the specific rules
governing adjudication in this match) Source: The Chess-Monthly, Vol.4, April
1883, ppn 237-238.} (19... a4 {(comments unless otherwise indicated are from
The Chess-Monthly, Vol.4, April 1883, p237) In adjudicating the position as
drawn the umpire based his decision on the following line of play:} 20. Rb2 {
Mr. Locock, when questioned by the umpire about his continuation, gave this
move. It is the only reply which averts decisive loss.} Nxc3 21. Bb4 $1 Ne2+
22. Kh1 Qh6 23. Bd2 {The only move.} Qh4 ({Black's best reply: after} 23...
Ng3+ 24. Kg1 {, Black would be compelled to draw by perpetual check.}) ({If} 
23... Qh5 $2 {White wins with} 24. Rf2 Ng3+ 25. Kg1 Nf5 26. g4) 24. f4 Re4 25.
g3 Qh3 {According to the regulations of the match, the series of six moves
would suffice to prove the correct­ness of the verdict, but it is easily
demonstrated that Black cannot acquire a winning superiority for a much longer
time.} 26. Rbb1 Nxd4 27. Rfe1 Rxe1+ ({If} 27... Rae8 {, White wins with} 28.
Qxd4 Re2 ({Stockfish 16:} 28... Rxe1+ $1 29. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 30. Bxe1 Qf1+ 31. Qg1
Qc4 $11) 29. Qf2 $1) 28. Rxe1 Nf5 29. Kg1 d4 30. Qa6 Rd8 31. Qxa4 Ne3 32. Qc6 {
and although Black has the advantage, the position even now would not justify
a judge to declare the game as won for Black. Source: The Chess-Monthly, April
1883, ppn 238. Contributed by Brian Denman, 5 May 2021. Corrected to show that
the moves after Black's 19th were analysis and not game moves, 4 September
2023.}) 1/2-1/2

