1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 Bb6 5. a4 a6 6. b5 axb5 7. Bxb5 Nd4 8. Bb2
Nxb5 9. axb5 Rxa1 10. Bxa1 d6 11. d4 exd4 12. Nxd4 Ne7 13. O-O O-O 14. Qh5 f5
15. Nd2 Qe8 16. Qg5 Qf7 17. Nxf5 Nxf5 18. exf5 Bxf5 19. c4 Be6 20. Kh1 Qd7 21.
Re1 Rf7 22. Ne4 Kf8 23. Qh4 h6 24. f4 c6 25. Ng5 $4 ({"The sacrifice of the
knight is unsound. This was demonstrated by Mr Zukertort, who watched play
carefully, immediately after the conclusion of the game." (The Field) (n.b.
Stockfish 10 (2019) finds the remarkable} 25. Nf6 $3 $18) 25... Bxc4 $4 ({
"We are surprised that Mr Raymond should have been too timid to accept the
proffered piece, and to have given up the exchange instead. The umpire pointed
out the following continuation:} 25... hxg5 26. Qh8+ Ke7 27. f5 (27. Bxg7 Rxf4
$19) ({If} 27. fxg5 {Black has a ready reply with} Qd8 28. Qh4 Kd7 29. Qe4 Bf5
{, etc (The Field)}) 27... Rxf5 28. Qxg7+ Kd8 29. Bf6+ Kc7 {with a won game." 
(The Field)}) 26. Nxf7 Bxf7 27. Qg3 Bh5 28. f5 Bd8 29. bxc6 bxc6 30. Re6 d5 31.
Rd6 Qc7 32. Bxg7+ Ke8 33. Be5 Qf7 34. Rxc6 Qxf5 35. Qg8+ Qf8 36. Re6+ Be7 37.
Rxe7+ {Source: The Field, 24 March 1883. "On Board 2 G.E. Wainwright 
(University) offered an Evans' Gambit to E.L. Raymond (Christ's), which the
latter prudently declined ... At 6.25 the President of the Dark Blues reaped
the fruits of his boldness. The game proceeded steadily for some hours, and
notwithstanding that the Cantab had to play all through a laborious defence,
his prospects were the better ones on account of his two Bishops, which were
bound to do good execution on the rather clear board, and the vastly superior
position of his Pawns. Mr. Wainwright tried to force matters by offering the
sacrifice of a Knight. The sacrifice was thoroughly unsound, for, after a
series of innocuous checks, Black would bring his King into perfect safety,
and come out with a piece for two worthless Pawns, and a fine attacking
position. Mr. Raymond failed to perceive the shallowness of the hostile scheme,
and, instead of capturing the piece, he sacrificed the exchange. The sequel
was played by the Oxonian in very good style." (Source: Chess-Monthly, Vol.4,
April 1883, p236)} 1-0

