{G.W. Küchler (Sidney) opened with 1 P to K4, which his opponent, S. Weall 
(St. John's), met with the French Defence ... A third victory for Cambridge
was scored by G.W. Küchler at 6 p.m. (and in BCM: "At Board No. 3 Mr. Kuchler
(Cambridge) had one stubborn game with Mr. Weall who followed " Gallic
strategy," i.e. played 1 ... P to K 3. The game proceeded in the old-fashioned
way 2 P to Q 4, P to Q 4, 3 P takes P, P takes P, but Black soon had a cramped
game owing to his Bishop occupying an assailable position at K 3. He won a
Pawn ; but gave White the chance of carrying on a strong " sacrificial "
attack on the K's side. Finally exchanges were made which left Black with a B
to a R, and after that his position was hopeless." (Chess Monthly) "Weall 
(Oxford) replied with the French defence, and made an indifferent move in
answer to 7.Kt to Kt 5 with P to KR3 - 7...Castles or Kt to B3 was preferable.
On the 11th move the Oxonian gained a pawn by a clever move, when the Cantab
sacrificed a piece, which, though unsound, complicated the position greatly,
and secured the game for Cambridge after 49 moves..." (The Field)} 1. e4 e6 2.
d4 d5 3. exd5 exd5 4. Nf3 Nf6 5. Bd3 Bd6 6. O-O Be6 7. Ne5 h6 8. f4 O-O 9. f5
Bd7 10. Nd2 Nc6 11. Ndf3 Nxd4 12. Nxd7 Nxf3+ 13. Qxf3 Qxd7 14. Qh3 Rfe8 15.
Bxh6 gxh6 16. Qxh6 Be5 17. Rf3 Nh7 18. f6 Nxf6 19. Rxf6 Bd4+ 20. Kh1 Re4 21.
Bxe4 dxe4 22. Rff1 Bxb2 23. Qg5+ Kf8 24. Rad1 Qe8 25. Rf4 e3 26. Qc5+ Kg8 27.
Rg4+ Bg7 28. Qg5 Qf8 29. Qxe3 Re8 30. Qg3 c6 31. h3 f6 32. Rdd4 Re7 33. Qb3+
Rf7 34. Rd7 Qe8 35. Qxf7+ Qxf7 36. Rxf7 Kxf7 37. Ra4 a6 38. Ra3 f5 39. Rb3 b5
40. Ra3 b4 41. Rxa6 c5 42. Ra7+ Kg6 43. Kh2 Bd4 44. Kg3 c4 45. Ra4 Bc5 46. Ra6+
Kh5 47. Rc6 Bd4 48. Rxc4 Bc3 49. Rxc3 {Source: Chess-Monthly, Vol.4, April
1883, p247} 1-0

