1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 Bb6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Qb3 O-O 7. Ng5 $2 Bxf2+
8. Kxf2 ({The sacrifice of the bishop does not seem to be sound. White need
not take the bishop; but may move} 8. Ke2 {, when Black would probably lose
the exchange. Instead of the text move, a more defensive course would have
been advisable." (The Field; n.b. Stockfish 10 thinks the B sacrifice is good,
but that Ng4 would have been slightly better. Hoffer's subsequent notes to
this game, suggesting improvements for White, make little or no sense since it
is clear that Black is already overwhelmingly better and White has zero
prospects of developing any counterplay.)}) 8... Ng4+ 9. Ke1 Qxg5 10. Rf1 Qg6
11. d3 Nxh2 12. Rf5 d5 13. Rg5 dxc4 14. Rxg6 cxb3 15. Rg3 Bg4 16. axb3 f5 17.
exf5 Rxf5 18. Nd2 Raf8 19. Ne4 Rf1+ 20. Kd2 R8f2+ 21. Nxf2 Rxf2+ 22. Ke1 Re2+
23. Kd1 Rxg2+ 24. Rxg4 Nxg4 25. Ra4 ({"This is quite useless. The game might
be abandoned at this stage; but if continued, why not} 25. b5 {at once?" (The
Field)}) 25... Nf2+ 26. Ke2 h5 27. b5 Ne7 28. Kf1 Rh2 29. Bg5 Nd5 30. Rxa7 Nxd3
31. Ra8+ Kh7 32. Rd8 c6 33. bxc6 bxc6 34. c4 N5b4 35. Rd7 h4 36. Be3 h3 37. Kg1
Re2 38. Ba7 c5 39. Rf7 Rg2+ 40. Kf1 Rg3 41. Rf5 Kg6 42. Rf8 h2 43. Rh8 Rg1+ 44.
Ke2 h1=Q {Source: The Field, 24 March 1883. "Opening proceedings on Board 4
were a repetition of those on Board 2; Rev. J.F. Welsh, B.A. (Christ Church)
invited an Evans', which was likewise declined by Rev. W.P. Buncombe, B.A. 
(Non. Coll.). ... Cambridge scored the first victory, 4.20, when Rev. Welsh
succumbed, after a hopelessly-prolonged resistance, to the vigorous onslaught
of Rev. Buncombe, who early in the opening got the superiority by a bold but
quite sound manoeuvre." (Chess Monthly - a much more succinct and accurate
assessment than that of The Field)} 0-1

