{"In the second game... Walker played the Evans Gambit, which Sherrard
defended regularly up to his 7th move, when instead of 7...P to Q3, he played
P takes P. This naturally ought to give the first player an easy-won game with
8. B takes P ch or Kt to Kt5, etc. White played 8. Q to Q5, giving his
opponent ample time to develop his game; though it might have been advisable
to bring out the knight to R3 prior to castling. White could not well exchange
this knight as it would have exposed his king side to a severe attack. Things
went on smoothly enough till the 18th move, when the Oxonian put his knight en
prise, after which oversight he showed great ingenuity and tenacity in
fighting an uphill battle, and his efforts were crowned with success. The
Cantab had the opportunity twice to retreat Q to Q2 on the 24th and 25th move,
followed by bringing his knight into play immediately with an easily won game;
but he paid the penalty for trusting too much to his numerical superiority." 
(The Field, 24 March 1883) - I'm wondering whether the opening might have
proceeded thus...} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 Bxb4 5. c3 Bc5 6. d4
exd4 7. O-O dxc3 8. Qd5 {... but cannot be sure I have the right moves - JS}
1-0

