{"In the second game, Lynam offered the Queen's Gambit, which was accepted by
Gwinner; but he judiciously refrained from defending the gambit pawn, and
quietly developed his game. The middle part was marked by a certain lack of
enterprise on the part of the Cantab, and an unnecessary eagerness to exchange
bishops for knights. When time was called the Cantab found himself on the 27th
move with a minus two knights against his adversaries two bishops and a
paralysed position. The umpire gave the game in favour of Oxford." (The Field)
} 1. d4 d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 Bg4 5. Nc3 e6 6. Bxc4 Bd6 7. h3 Bh5 8.
Bd2 O-O 9. Rc1 c6 10. Be2 h6 11. Rc2 Nbd7 12. e4 Bxf3 13. Bxf3 e5 14. Be3 Bb4
15. O-O Re8 16. Qd3 Qc7 17. Qc4 Bxc3 18. Rxc3 Nb6 19. Qd3 Rad8 20. Rd1 Nbd5 21.
Rc4 Ne7 22. Qc3 Ng6 23. g3 Qc8 24. Kg2 Re6 25. d5 Red6 26. Bxa7 h5 27. Be3 {
Adjudicated win for White. Source: Chess-Monthly, Vol.4, April 1883, p238.} 1-0

